WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:06.907
♪♪

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.475
&gt;&gt;&gt; So we're learning some new

00:00:08.608 --> 00:00:10.043
details about the objects that

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.611
were shot down by U.S. Fighter

00:00:12.045 --> 00:00:12.812
jets over the weekend.

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:14.080
This is according to a Pentagon

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:15.448
memo obtained by CNN.

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.417
The omts shot down over Alaska

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.452
was metallic and the size of a

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:20.520
small car.

00:00:20.654 --> 00:00:22.989
The object shot down over Canada

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:24.858
was a small, metallic balloon

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:26.326
that crossed near sensitive

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.061
sights in the U.S. Before it was

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:29.296
shot down and the object shot

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:31.531
down over lake Huron on Sunday

00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:34.067
slowly descended into the water

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:34.701
after impact.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.537
Now, the objects were flying at

00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:39.005
altitudes between 20 to 40,000

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:39.306
feet.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:40.740
Military decided to shoot them

00:00:40.874 --> 00:00:43.343
down because at those altitudes

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:44.611
they could pose a threat to

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.313
civilian aircraft, which would

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:48.481
be really, really serious.

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:50.350
Joining us now, white house

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:51.451
national security counsel

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:52.452
coordinator for strategic

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.753
communication and that's John

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:54.187
Kirby.

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.088
John Kirby, thank you for

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.089
joining us this morning.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:56.890
I appreciate it.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:58.391
&gt;&gt; You bet.

00:00:58.525 --> 00:00:58.892
Happy to be here.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:00.126
&gt;&gt; Since we last officially

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.394
heard from you, any briefing or

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.130
any update that we should know?

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.099
&gt;&gt; Nothing I can say

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:06.499
operationally, don.

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:07.767
We still have not located the

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.369
debris from the three objects

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.338
shot down over the weekend.

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:12.205
Although, there are intensive

00:01:12.339 --> 00:01:14.641
recovery operations under way to

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.109
try to find, locate that.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.410
Once it's been located to try to

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:18.111
recover that.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.245
But nothing to report.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.147
Now on the spy balloon that was

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:23.383
shot down a week or so ago,

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.951
divers were able to get into the

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:26.119
water over the course of the

00:01:26.119 --> 00:01:27.253
weekend and were able to recover

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.356
a significant amount of debris,

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:30.790
including some of the structure

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.125
and some of the electronics.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.560
All that has been shipped off

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:34.728
now with our FBI partners.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.129
We're going to try to analyze

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:37.530
that, see what we can learn.

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.632
&gt;&gt; It almost seems like this is

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.700
something novel and now the

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.369
United States and I know it is

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:42.635
not.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:44.137
Should it take the U.S. Military

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:45.138
this long to recover these

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.439
objects especially if the U.S.

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:48.608
Is the one that shot them down,

00:01:48.742 --> 00:01:48.908
John?

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.143
&gt;&gt; You know, when you do

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:52.178
something like this, don, you

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.680
want to do it over areas where

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.181
you're not going to hurt people

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.216
on the ground which means it

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:57.684
will be remote areas as best you

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:58.151
can.

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.519
Then just getting to those

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:00.920
remote areas can be difficult,

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:01.287
don.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.157
Particularly if you throw in not

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:06.393
only the latitude at which these

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:07.527
things are located, at least the

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.595
first two but the weather

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:10.230
conditions right now.

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.999
It's February and up near the

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.167
arctic, the weather conditions

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.236
are not permissive for search

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.203
and recovery operation.

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:19.272
&gt;&gt; This is fascinating to folks

00:02:19.406 --> 00:02:21.074
just watching at home.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.509
Everyone is wondering, what's

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:23.209
going on?

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:24.177
Why so many.

00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:24.611
Is this new.

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.980
If you're looking for more, I'm

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:27.814
paraphrasing here, you're likely

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:28.782
to find them.

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.517
Is that what you believe?

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.318
Is that what -- is that what our

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.787
intelligence folks believe?

00:02:34.187 --> 00:02:36.189
&gt;&gt; We think in part, don, that

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:37.323
this could explain why you've

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.592
seen so many incidents in a

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:40.593
short period of time just over

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:42.862
the last few days.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:47.300
We adjusted the norad staff

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:48.835
adjusted the parameters for the

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:50.670
radar systems.

00:02:50.804 --> 00:02:52.806
The sensitivities to look for

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:54.307
things that were low -- I'm

00:02:54.441 --> 00:02:57.377
sorry, slow, high, and small.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.845
Small radar cross section in the

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.413
wake of the Chinese spy balloon.

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:02.715
So when you're doing that, when

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.918
you adjust your sensitivities on

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:06.586
the radar, you're likely to see

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.621
more of those kinds of contact.

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:10.256
We think that's in part why

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.691
there have been these most

00:03:11.825 --> 00:03:13.293
recent incidents.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.361
&gt;&gt; CNN also learned the first

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.130
missile launched at the object

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.798
over lake Huron did not hit the

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:19.132
target.

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.667
Do you know where that missile

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:20.934
is?

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.402
Does it concern you if missiles

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.271
are missing targets that over

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:27.307
American people, over, you know,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.241
the United States?

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.709
&gt;&gt; I can't confirm the reporting

00:03:29.843 --> 00:03:32.512
that a missile was fired and

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:32.846
missed.

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.247
I would add a couple of thoughts

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:34.380
here.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:37.250
One again, these were very small

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.885
radar cross section targets.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:41.721
And so, it's not inconceivable

00:03:41.855 --> 00:03:45.492
to me as a former military man

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:46.926
to believe that one might have

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.229
missed just because of the size

00:03:49.362 --> 00:03:50.263
of the target.

00:03:50.263 --> 00:03:51.464
And there are safety mechanisms

00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:54.267
in place that pilots use.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:54.701
I don't think the American

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:55.935
people or the Canadian people

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.137
need to be worried about the

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.338
possibility that one of those

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:58.671
missed.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:03:59.806
&gt;&gt; But that is our reporting

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.141
that it did not hit its target

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:02.375
and this is happening over

00:04:02.509 --> 00:04:03.143
American air space.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:04.777
If the standard operating

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:05.879
procedure going forward is to

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:07.847
shoot these objects down to keep

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:09.182
civilian aircraft safe in the

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.818
sky, can you guarantee Americans

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.052
on the ground that Americans on

00:04:13.186 --> 00:04:14.287
the ground will also stay safe

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:15.255
as well, John?

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:17.624
&gt;&gt; Yes, I can, don.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:19.125
&gt;&gt; As simple as that, even

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:21.394
though you said you don't know

00:04:21.528 --> 00:04:23.329
if it was -- where that missile

00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:25.031
is and you can't confirm, but

00:04:25.165 --> 00:04:26.299
that is your reporting?

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:27.567
&gt;&gt; I can't confirm your

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:27.934
reporting.

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:29.102
There are safety protocols with

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.871
all these kinds of engagements.

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:34.307
The whole purpose of these

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:36.576
missions were to keep Americans

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:36.876
safe.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:37.644
That's what was behind the

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:38.745
president's decision from the

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:39.913
very, very beginning.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:41.314
Make sure we were keeping the

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.849
skies safe for civilian air

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:43.183
traffic.

00:04:43.316 --> 00:04:45.084
These three objects were at

00:04:45.218 --> 00:04:46.319
altitudes that could potentially

00:04:46.319 --> 00:04:47.921
pose a risk to commercial air

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:48.421
traffic.

00:04:48.555 --> 00:04:51.090
And also, they pose a potential

00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:53.293
surveillance risk to sensitive

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:54.327
sites on the ground.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.595
It's all about safety and

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:56.429
security of American people.

